WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:04.630
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:10.139
3D printing is a rapidly progressing research
field. One can create colorful patterns that

00:00:10.139 --> 00:00:15.259
we call textures on different figures with
Computational Hydrographic Printing, just

00:00:15.259 --> 00:00:17.710
to name one of many recent inventions.

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:22.480
We can 3D print teeth, action figures, prosthetics,
you name it.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:28.259
Ever thought of how cool it would be to design
robots on your computer digitally and simply

00:00:28.259 --> 00:00:33.220
printing them. Scientists at Disney Research
had just made this dream come true.

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:38.880
I fondly remember my time working at Disney
Research, where robots like these were walking

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:45.320
about. I remember a specific guy, that, well,
wasn't really kind and waved at me, it has

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:49.980
blocked the path to one of the labs I had
to enter. It might have been one of these

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:55.640
guys in this project. Disney Research has
an incredible atmosphere with so many talented

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:58.680
people, it's an absolutely amazing place.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:04.739
So, in order to get a robot from A to B, one
has to specify scientific attributes like

00:01:04.739 --> 00:01:11.860
trajectories and angular velocities. But people
don't think in angular velocities, they think

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:19.220
in intuitive actions, like moving forward,
sideways, or even the style of a desired movement.

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:23.050
Specifying these things instead would be much
more useful.

00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:29.000
That sounds great and all, but this is a quite
difficult task. If one specifies a high level

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:35.880
action, like walking sideways, then the algorithm
has to find out what body parts to move, how,

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:40.760
which motors should be turned on and when,
which joints to turn, where is the center

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:47.090
of pressure, center of mass, and many other
factors have to be taken into consideration.

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:52.229
This technique offers a really slick solution
to this, where we don't just get a good result,

00:01:52.229 --> 00:01:57.630
but we can also have our say on what should
the order of steps be. And even more, our

00:01:57.630 --> 00:02:09.229
stylistic suggestions are taken into consideration.

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:14.659
One can also change the design of the robot,
for instance, different shapes, motor positions,

00:02:14.659 --> 00:02:16.920
and joints can be specified.

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:21.900
The authors ran a simulation for these designs
and constraints, and found them to be in good

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:28.459
agreement with reality. This means that whatever
you design digitally can be 3D printed with

00:02:28.459 --> 00:02:33.709
off the shelf parts and brought to life, just
as you see them on the screen.

00:02:33.709 --> 00:02:38.400
The technique supports an arbitrary number
of legs and is robust to a number of different

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:43.930
robot designs. Amazing is as good of a word as I can find.

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:49.409
The kids of the future will be absolutely
spoiled with their toys, that's for sure,

00:02:49.409 --> 00:02:53.549
and I'm perfectly convinced that there will
be many other other applications, and these

00:02:53.549 --> 00:02:59.200
guys will help us solve problems that are
currently absolutely inconceivable for us.

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:03.099
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

